title: Oracle EBS / Cl0p
page: breaches/oracle-ebs-cl0p.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Cl0p exploited CVE-2025-61882, an unauthenticated Oracle EBS flaw, from around August 2025, roughly two months before Oracle's October 4 patch; many victims first heard of it in an extortion email.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 9.8 | CVSS score | CVE-2025-61882: unauthenticated, remote, no user interaction
stat: ~2 | months of exploitation before a patch existed | from around August 2025 to Oracle's October 4 patch
stat: 70,000 | US dollars, roughly: the advertised price of an EBS zero-day | in dark-web posts in June 2025
No encryption: data theft and extortion only. No credential was stolen, no employee was tricked, and no token was forged.
notes:
Start with the figures the page leads with.

CVE-2025-61882 is an unauthenticated remote code execution flaw in Oracle E-Business Suite, scored 9.8 on CVSS. Unauthenticated means it is reachable without any credential. Remote code execution means an attacker runs code of their choosing on someone else's server. The background slide after stage 02 explains CVE and CVSS.

The Cl0p extortion crew began exploiting it as a zero-day around August 2025. A zero-day is a flaw attackers use before the vendor knows about it. Oracle published its security alert and emergency patch on October 4, so for roughly two months there was no advisory to act on and no patch to apply.

The third figure comes from June 2025, when dark-web posts advertised an EBS zero-day at around $70,000. That is the page's wording for the listing: an EBS zero-day, at about that price.

EBS is Oracle E-Business Suite, an ERP system. ERP stands for enterprise resource planning: software a company runs its core business records and processes on.

Nothing was encrypted. The page describes this chain as data theft and extortion only, without ever deploying ransomware.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: June 2025 | Dark-web posts advertise an Oracle EBS zero-day at about $70,000
event: Around August 2025 | Cl0p begins exploiting the EBS flaw as a zero-day
event: Before the advisory, for many | Extortion emails reach executives, claiming data theft
event: October 4, 2025 | Oracle publishes its security alert and emergency patch
event: Early October | An exploit bundle leaks publicly
event: October 6 | CISA adds the CVE to its KEV catalog
The page gives months for the first two events and no date for the emails. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives these dates, and the timeline shows only those.

In June 2025, dark-web posts advertised an Oracle EBS zero-day at around $70,000, roughly two months before mass exploitation began. Around August 2025, Cl0p began exploiting CVE-2025-61882 in the wild.

For many victims, the first sign of anything was an extortion email to their executives, claiming data theft from their EBS systems. It arrived before Oracle's advisory. The page gives no date for the emails, so the slide places them only as the page does.

On October 4, 2025, Oracle published its security alert and an out-of-cycle patch. Out of cycle means outside the vendor's regular patch schedule. On October 6, CISA added the flaw to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog.

In the same period an exploit bundle leaked publicly. The page's heading for stage 07 puts the leak between the patch and the KEV listing, and the stage itself says early October.

The markers are evenly spaced, so read them as order only.

== Who said what
layout: table
row: Who | What the page reports
row: Oracle | Security alert and out-of-cycle patch, October 4, 2025
row: CISA | Added it to KEV on October 6, confirming active use in ransomware-adjacent campaigns
row: Cl0p | Emails to executives claiming data theft from their EBS environments
row: Researchers | Not fully established whether the leaked code exploits this CVE or an older n-day
The page's primary sources are Oracle, Rapid7, Tenable, Oligo Security and MITRE.
notes:
Four parties speak in this story, each about something different.

Oracle published its security alert and an out-of-cycle patch for CVE-2025-61882 on October 4, 2025. CISA, the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, added it to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on October 6, confirming active use in ransomware-adjacent campaigns.

Cl0p reached many victims first: emails to executives claiming data theft from their EBS environments, with leak-site publication as the deadline mechanism.

Researchers noted that it was not fully established whether the leaked exploit code targets CVE-2025-61882 itself or an older n-day. An n-day is a flaw that has already been publicly disclosed. Keep that caveat whenever you mention the leak.

The page's primary sources are Oracle's security alert, Rapid7, Tenable's FAQ, Oligo Security and MITRE's page for T1190. They are listed at the end of the deck.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Resource Development | A zero-day with a price tag | T1588.006
step: 02 | Initial Access | No credential required | T1190
step: 03 | Execution | SSRF to XSLT to the shell | T1059
step: 04 | Collection | Everything worth extorting over | T1213
step: 05 | Exfiltration | Quiet, for weeks | T1041
step: 06 | Impact | Extortion email first, for many | T1657
step: 07 | Response | Patch, KEV, leaked exploit | T1588.005
Arrows follow the page's stage order.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase and primary ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is the zero-day with a price tag, advertised on dark-web posts in June 2025. Stage 02 is the way in, with no credential required. Stage 03 is execution, from a fetched stylesheet to operating-system commands. Stage 04 is what an ERP holds. Stage 05 is the quiet exfiltration, over weeks. Stage 06 is the impact: an extortion email that, for many victims, arrived before any advisory. Stage 07 is the response: Oracle's patch, CISA's listing, and an exploit bundle that leaked in the same period.

The arrows follow the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Dark-web posts priced an Oracle EBS zero-day at about $70,000
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Resource Development
technique: T1588.006 | Obtain Capabilities: Vulnerabilities
fact: June 2025 | an Oracle EBS zero-day listed at roughly $70,000
fact: August 2025 | Cl0p begins exploitation in the wild
fact: Economics | a five-figure purchase against revenue-sized extortion demands
fact: Exploit markets | intelligence on them is sometimes the only pre-patch warning
Before any of this was a security advisory, the page says, it was a listing.
notes:
Stage 01 is where the capability came from. The page maps it to T1588.006, Obtain Capabilities: Vulnerabilities.

Before any of this was a security advisory, the page says, it was a listing. In June 2025, posts on criminal forums offered an Oracle EBS zero-day at around seventy thousand dollars, roughly two months before mass exploitation began.

The page sets that price against what followed: data theft from many large enterprises, and extortion demands sized to their revenue. On those economics, it calls an unauthenticated flaw in widely deployed enterprise software one of the highest-return purchases available to a crew like Cl0p. In its summary, the page describes Cl0p's model as acquiring or finding such a flaw.

The listing also matters to defenders as a timing signal. Threat-intelligence coverage of exploit markets, the page says, is occasionally the only warning that exists during the window when no patch does.

None of the page's six defenses cites this stage. That comes back in the discussion.

The next slide explains, in general terms, how ATT&CK describes obtaining vulnerabilities.

== What ATT&CK calls obtaining vulnerabilities
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1588.006 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1588/006/
ATT&CK says adversaries may acquire information about vulnerabilities to use during targeting. There is usually a delay between a vulnerability's discovery and its public disclosure.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files this under Obtain Capabilities: Vulnerabilities. Adversaries may acquire information about vulnerabilities that can be used during targeting. ATT&CK defines a vulnerability as a weakness in computer hardware or software that can, potentially, be exploited by an adversary to cause unintended or unanticipated behavior.

Adversaries may find vulnerability information by searching open databases or by gaining access to closed vulnerability databases. They may monitor vulnerability disclosures to understand the state of existing, as well as newly discovered, vulnerabilities. ATT&CK notes that there is usually a delay between when a vulnerability is discovered and when it is made public.

Knowledge of a vulnerability may cause an adversary to search for an existing exploit, or to attempt to develop one.

Nothing here describes the incident. The page says only that dark-web posts advertised an Oracle EBS zero-day for sale in June 2025.

== Network reach was the entire prerequisite, on EBS 12.2.3 through 12.2.14
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
fact: CVE | CVE-2025-61882, CVSS 9.8
fact: Component | Oracle Concurrent Processing, via the BI Publisher Integration
fact: Entry point | EBS configuration endpoints, configurator / UiServlet flow
fact: Prerequisites | network reachability; no credential, no user interaction
Patched, vendor-supported ERP was not sufficient: the vendor did not yet know.
notes:
Stage 02 is the way in. The page maps it to T1190, Exploit Public-Facing Application.

The page says everything that makes this severe is in the word unauthenticated. There is no phishing step, no credential to steal, no MFA to defeat and no insider to recruit. MFA is multi-factor authentication. Reaching the endpoint over the network is the entire prerequisite.

The vulnerable component is Oracle Concurrent Processing, associated with the BI Publisher Integration, reached through EBS configuration endpoints in the configurator and UiServlet flow. Affected versions run from 12.2.3 through 12.2.14, which the page calls essentially the supported 12.2 line rather than some neglected legacy release.

So the organizations hit were not running unsupported software. They were running current, patched, vendor-supported ERP, and that was not sufficient, because the vendor did not yet know.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what a CVE and a CVSS score are.

== What a CVE and a CVSS score are
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, CVSS | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-cvss
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry CVE
A CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) is a unique ID for a publicly disclosed vulnerability. CVSS, the Common Vulnerability Scoring System, gives a CVE a severity score from 0 to 10.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains two general terms from the csoh.org glossary.

The glossary defines a CVE, from Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures, as a unique ID for a publicly disclosed vulnerability, assigned by MITRE or a CNA. CNA stands for CVE Numbering Authority, an organization authorized to assign CVE IDs.

It defines CVSS, the Common Vulnerability Scoring System, as a 0 to 10 severity score for a CVE. It adds that CVSS correlates poorly with actual exploitation. The background slide after stage 07 covers a list that tracks exploitation itself.

Nothing here adds to the incident. The page gives CVE-2025-61882 a CVSS score of 9.8, and describes it as unauthenticated, remote and needing no user interaction.

Also from the csoh.org glossary entry CVE: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-cve

== SSRF into XSLT into a script engine into the shell
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Execution
technique: T1059 | Command and Scripting Interpreter
technique: T1190 | Exploit Public-Facing Application
fact: Fetched | an attacker-hosted XSL stylesheet
fact: Evaluated | Base64-encoded JavaScript, by a Java ScriptEngine
fact: Result | operating-system commands, run as the application user
fact: Breaks link 1 | egress filtering, so the server cannot fetch the stylesheet
Four legitimate, documented features, composed into remote code execution.
notes:
Stage 03 is execution. The page maps it to T1059, Command and Scripting Interpreter, and tags T1190 again, Exploit Public-Facing Application.

A crafted request made the EBS server fetch an attacker-hosted XSL stylesheet. The server's XSLT processor instantiated a ScriptEngine that evaluated Base64 JavaScript, and that JavaScript called Runtime.getRuntime().exec() to run operating-system commands.

XSLT is a language for transforming XML documents, and an XSL stylesheet is a document written in it. A ScriptEngine is Java's built-in way to run code written in a scripting language such as JavaScript. Base64 is an encoding that writes any data as letters, digits and a few symbols.

The page calls it a good teaching example because no single link is exotic: the exploit is assembled from ordinary enterprise-Java behaviour. Four legitimate features, each individually documented, composed into unauthenticated remote code execution.

The next slide draws the four links in order.

== The four links, in the page's order
layout: layers
layer: Link 1: SSRF | The server fetches an attacker-hosted XSL stylesheet
layer: Link 2: XSLT injection | The stylesheet is processed as instructions, not data
layer: Link 3: ScriptEngine | Base64-encoded JavaScript from the stylesheet is evaluated
layer: Link 4: Runtime exec | Operating-system commands run as the application user
Egress filtering breaks link 1, the page says, so the server cannot fetch the stylesheet at all.
notes:
This slide draws the chain the page walks through, link by link.

First, server-side request forgery: the payload points at a stylesheet URL, and the EBS server fetches it from attacker infrastructure. Second, XSLT injection: the server-side transformation engine processes that stylesheet as instructions rather than data. Third, the transformation instantiates a Java ScriptEngine and evaluates Base64-encoded JavaScript embedded in the stylesheet. Fourth, that JavaScript reaches java.lang.Runtime.getRuntime().exec and runs operating-system commands as the application user.

The arrows follow that order, from each link to the next.

Notice that link 1 requires the server to make an outbound connection. That, the page says, is why egress filtering on an ERP host is not paranoia. It comes back in the defenses.

The next slide explains server-side request forgery in general terms.

== What server-side request forgery is
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, SSRF | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-ssrf
credit: Also from the OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet (cheatsheetseries.owasp.org)
The csoh.org glossary defines server-side request forgery (SSRF) as tricking a server into fetching an attacker-chosen URL. OWASP says it abuses an application to interact with the internal or external network, or the machine itself.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general idea from the csoh.org glossary and OWASP.

The glossary defines SSRF, server-side request forgery, as tricking a server into fetching an attacker-chosen URL.

OWASP's SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet describes SSRF as an attack vector that abuses an application to interact with the internal or external network, or the machine itself. It names the mishandling of URLs as one of the enablers, with examples such as an application downloading an image from a URL a user enters, or a webhook or callback URL a user specifies.

Nothing here describes the incident beyond the page. The page says the payload pointed at a stylesheet URL, and the EBS server fetched it from attacker infrastructure.

Also from the OWASP SSRF Prevention Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Server_Side_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html

== An ERP holds everything worth extorting over
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Collection
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
fact: Data reachable | payroll, HR, financials, supplier contracts, customer data
fact: Why the target | most sensitive records, consolidated into ERP by design
fact: Extortion leverage | regulated, commercially sensitive, personally identifiable
fact: Recurring tension | the crown-jewel system also has the internet-facing portal
One foothold yields a collection that makes an extortion demand credible without any need to encrypt a thing.
notes:
Stage 04 is what the foothold reached. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories.

Code execution on an EBS server reaches payroll, HR records, financials, supplier contracts and customer data in one place. The page says Cl0p's target selection is not accidental. An ERP is the system an enterprise deliberately consolidates its most sensitive records into: employee personal data and payroll, financial reporting, supplier contracts and pricing, and customer information.

So one foothold yields a collection that is regulated, commercially sensitive and personally identifiable at the same time. The page calls that exactly the combination that makes an extortion demand credible without any need to encrypt a thing.

The page adds that the crown jewels framing in a data-classification exercise, meaning the short list of systems an organization most needs to protect, has to survive contact with reality. The ERP is usually on that list, the page says, and just as usually reachable from the internet, because a supplier portal or a remote finance team needs it.

== Quiet, and for weeks, with nothing encrypted
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1041 | Exfiltration Over C2 Channel
fact: Start | around August 2025, before any advisory existed
fact: Not used | ransomware, encryption, or any deliberately visible disruption
fact: Why quiet | visible impact ends the access, and the access is the asset
fact: Best available signal | outbound volume from an otherwise highly predictable host
The detection burden falls entirely on outbound data-flow monitoring.
notes:
Stage 05 is the harvest. The page maps it to T1041, Exfiltration Over C2 Channel. C2 stands for command and control.

Data was taken over an extended period beginning in August, with no ransomware deployed and nothing to alert the victim that anything had happened. Nothing was encrypted, no ransom note appeared on a screen, and no service went down. The page explains why: visible disruption would end the access, and the access is the asset.

The crew harvests steadily from many victims at once and keeps quiet. So the detection burden falls entirely on outbound data-flow monitoring, from a server that nobody expects to initiate large transfers.

The page describes an ERP application host's network profile as highly predictable: it talks to its database, to a handful of internal integrations, and to whatever portal fronts it. Sustained outbound volume to an unfamiliar destination is about as clear an anomaly as that environment can produce. It is visible only to someone who baselined the normal case in advance.

== For many, the extortion email arrived first
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Delivery | emails to executives, claiming data theft from EBS
fact: Timing | for many organizations, before Oracle's advisory existed
fact: Pressure | leak-site publication as the deadline mechanism
fact: Same playbook | Accellion FTA, GoAnywhere MFT, MOVEit Transfer
Your incident response process must handle "the criminal told us first."
notes:
Stage 06 is the impact. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft.

Executives at affected organizations received emails from Cl0p claiming data theft from their EBS systems, in many cases before anyone knew a vulnerability existed. For many victims, the page says, the notification chain ran backwards. The email to leadership was the first indication of anything, arriving before Oracle's advisory, and so before any internal patching conversation could have started.

Consider what that does to an incident response process built on the assumption that detection precedes disclosure. The security team is asked to confirm or refute a criminal's claim about a system it has no reason to suspect, with no CVE to reference, no indicators published, and executives already reading a ransom demand. Your process, the page says, must handle the criminal telling you first.

The page says Cl0p has run this pattern repeatedly: Accellion FTA, GoAnywhere MFT and MOVEit Transfer. FTA stands for File Transfer Appliance and MFT for managed file transfer. Each time the distinguishing feature is the same: mass exploitation of one unauthenticated flaw, a long quiet harvest, then simultaneous extortion of everyone at once.

The next slide explains, in general terms, why ATT&CK files extortion under Financial Theft.

== Why ATT&CK files extortion under Financial Theft
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1657 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1657/
ATT&CK says adversaries may steal money from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft or other methods. Its ransomware example adds a threat to leak sensitive data unless paid. The page says this campaign deployed no ransomware.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

Financial Theft sits under the Impact tactic. ATT&CK says adversaries may steal monetary resources from targets through extortion, social engineering, technical theft, or other methods aimed at their own financial gain. It calls financial theft the ultimate objective of several popular campaign types, including extortion by ransomware, business email compromise and fraud.

ATT&CK's extortion example is ransomware. An adversary demands payment from a victim after encrypting data and exfiltrating it, then threatens to leak sensitive data to the public unless payment is made. Adversaries may use dedicated leak sites to distribute victim data.

Keep the difference in mind. ATT&CK's example includes encryption. The page says this campaign encrypted nothing and never deployed ransomware: it was data theft and extortion only.

== Patch on October 4, KEV on October 6, an exploit leaked between
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Response
technique: T1588.005 | Obtain Capabilities: Exploits
fact: October 4, 2025 | Oracle security alert and emergency out-of-cycle patch
fact: Early October | leaked exploit bundle: exp.py, server.py and a readme
fact: Unresolved | whether the leaked code targets this CVE or an older n-day
fact: Also required | threat hunting back to August, when exploitation began
Once a patch exists, the flaw becomes usable by everyone, the page says.
notes:
Stage 07 is the response. The page maps it to T1588.005, Obtain Capabilities: Exploits.

Oracle published its security alert and out-of-cycle patch for CVE-2025-61882 on October 4, 2025. CISA added it to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on October 6, confirming active use in ransomware-adjacent campaigns.

In the same period an exploit bundle leaked publicly, containing exp.py, server.py and a readme with setup instructions. Researchers noted it was not fully established whether that code exploited CVE-2025-61882 itself or an older n-day. Keep that caveat.

Either way, the page says, the practical effect on defenders is identical: the familiar post-disclosure squeeze. The moment a patch exists, the flaw becomes usable by everyone rather than only by whoever paid for it, and the interval in which patching is optional closes immediately.

Anyone patching on October 4 also had to assume compromise dating back to August, because the exploitation predates the advisory by two months. Threat hunting is a search through your own telemetry for attacker activity that never raised an alert.

The next slide explains, in general terms, what CISA's KEV catalog is.

== What CISA's KEV catalog is
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, KEV | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-kev
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entries CISA and CISA KEV
The csoh.org glossary defines KEV, Known Exploited Vulnerabilities, as CISA's catalog of CVEs known to be actively exploited: the "fix this now" list. CISA is the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency.
notes:
This is a background slide. It explains a general term from the csoh.org glossary.

The glossary defines KEV, Known Exploited Vulnerabilities, as CISA's catalog of CVEs known to be actively exploited, and calls it the fix this now list. Its CISA KEV entry calls the catalog the patch this now list for vulnerabilities seen in real attacks. CISA is the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, which also publishes advisories and Secure-by-Design guidance.

Set it beside the background after stage 02. The glossary calls CVSS a severity score that correlates poorly with actual exploitation. A KEV listing says a flaw is known to be exploited.

Nothing here adds to the incident. The page says CISA added CVE-2025-61882 to the catalog on October 6, two days after Oracle's patch.

Also from the csoh.org glossary entry CISA: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-cisa

And from the csoh.org glossary entry CISA KEV: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-cisa-kev

== One unauthenticated flaw, a quiet harvest, then simultaneous extortion
layout: compare
col: Not in this chain | No stolen credential | No tricked employee | No forged token | No ransomware
col: Cl0p's industrial model | Acquire or find an unauthenticated flaw in software deployed at thousands of large enterprises | Harvest quietly at scale | Extort months later, by email and a leak site
The page names Accellion FTA, GoAnywhere MFT and MOVEit Transfer as precedents: the same playbook each time.
notes:
The page draws one pattern from this chain.

It calls the chain the counterweight to most of the identity-driven incidents among the site's breach kill chains: no credential was stolen, no employee was tricked, and no token was forged.

Cl0p's model, in the page's word, is industrial. Acquire or find an unauthenticated flaw in software deployed at thousands of large enterprises, harvest quietly at scale, then monetize months later through email extortion and a leak site, without ever deploying ransomware.

The page says Cl0p has run this pattern repeatedly, with Accellion FTA, GoAnywhere MFT and MOVEit Transfer. Each time, the distinguishing feature is the same: mass exploitation of one unauthenticated flaw, a long quiet harvest, then simultaneous extortion of everyone at once.

The defenses come next.

== Six defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Get the ERP off the internet | 02
row: Filter egress from application servers | 03
row: Rehearse an emergency patching path | 07
row: Baseline outbound data flows | 05
row: Minimize what the crown-jewel system stores | 04
row: Write the extortion path into the incident response plan | 06
No defense on the page cites a fact from stage 01.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the seven stages. The defenses name no stage numbers, so a cell is marked only where a defense's own words cite that stage's facts.

Getting the ERP off the internet cites the unauthenticated internet attacker of stage 02. Egress filtering breaks the exploit at its first link, the stylesheet fetch in stage 03. The emergency patching path cites Oracle's out-of-cycle patch and the KEV listing two days later, stage 07. Baselining outbound flows cites a harvest in which nothing was encrypted and nothing broke, stage 05. Minimizing what the crown-jewel system stores cites the concentration of regulated data, stage 04. The extortion path cites the email to an executive with no CVE published, stage 06.

Each defense is marked once. Some mention another stage in passing, as the minimizing defense names exfiltration as what it limits; a passing mention is not marked.

No defense cites a fact from stage 01, the advertised zero-day. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Off the internet, and no arbitrary egress
layout: compare
col: ERP off the internet (stage 02) | Reachable only via VPN or identity-aware proxy: not exploitable by unauthenticated internet attackers | Enumerate what of your ERP estate is internet-facing, and why | Put the answer behind authentication | Treat any remaining public endpoint as a documented, monitored exception
col: Filter egress (stage 03) | An ERP host has no legitimate need for arbitrary outbound HTTP | Default-deny egress, with a narrow allow-list | Stops the SSRF from ever retrieving a payload | Needs no knowledge of the flaw
The page frames both for the window when nobody knew about the flaw.
notes:
Two defenses come straight from stages 02 and 03.

First, get the ERP off the internet. The page calls attack-surface reduction the only control that works before a patch exists: every other measure on its list depends on knowing about the flaw, and for two months nobody did. An EBS instance reachable only through a VPN or an identity-aware proxy was not exploitable by an unauthenticated internet attacker, regardless of the zero-day. An identity-aware proxy is a gateway that checks who is asking before a request reaches an internal application. Enumerate what of your ERP estate is internet-facing and why, put the answer behind authentication, and treat any remaining public endpoint as a deliberate, documented, monitored exception.

Second, filter egress from application servers, which the page says breaks the exploit at its first link. Egress is outbound traffic. The chain begins with the EBS server fetching an attacker-hosted stylesheet, and an ERP application host has no legitimate need to make arbitrary outbound HTTP requests to the internet. Default-deny egress, with a narrow allow-list for the destinations it genuinely requires, stops the SSRF from ever retrieving a payload. It does so without any knowledge of the vulnerability, which the page calls the property you want from a control during an unknown zero-day.

== Rehearse the patch, and the extortion email
layout: compare
col: Emergency patching (stage 07) | Oracle shipped out of cycle; CISA had it in KEV two days later | Patching only in a quarterly window left ERP exposed for months afterwards | Decide who authorizes an emergency change to the finance system | Decide the rollback and how long it takes; measure it in a drill
col: The extortion path (stage 06) | Assume the first notice is an executive's email, with no CVE published | Decide who validates the claim and how you tell what left | Decide when legal and regulatory notification clocks start | Decide who communicates, and the position on paying, in advance
Both ask you to decide in advance, then rehearse.
notes:
Two defenses ask you to decide things before you need them.

First, have a real emergency patching path, and rehearse it on an ERP. Oracle shipped this patch out of cycle, and CISA had it in KEV two days later. Organizations that can only patch a business-critical ERP during a quarterly maintenance window, the page says, were exposed for months afterwards, on top of the two before. Decide in advance who authorizes an emergency change to the finance system, what the rollback looks like and how long it actually takes, then measure it during a drill rather than during an incident.

Second, write the extortion path into your incident response plan explicitly. Assume the first notification arrives as an email to an executive naming a system you have no alert for, with no CVE published. Decide now who validates the claim, how you determine what actually left, when legal and regulatory notification clocks start, and who is authorized to communicate. Also decide the position on paying before a deadline is in front of you.

The page points to the site's incident response page for the general shape, and says this variant needs its own rehearsal.

== Baseline outbound flows; store less
layout: compare
col: Baseline outbound (stage 05) | Volume was the only reliable signal: nothing was encrypted or broke | Alert on sustained transfers that do not fit the ERP's profile | Monitor bulk database reads no scheduled job explains | Keep logs where an attacker on the host cannot edit them
col: Store less (stage 04) | Cl0p's leverage came from regulated data concentrated in one reachable place | Purge historical records past their retention obligation | Tokenize or vault the highest-sensitivity fields | Segment, so reaching the app does not automatically expose all data
Exfiltration cannot take what was never there, the page says.
notes:
The last two defenses cover the harvest and what it could take.

Baseline outbound data flows, so a quiet harvest has something to trip. Nothing was encrypted and nothing broke, so the page calls volume the only reliable signal. Instrument the ERP's network profile, meaning which destinations, what size and at what times, and alert on sustained transfers that do not fit. Pair it with database-level monitoring for bulk reads that no scheduled job explains, and keep those logs somewhere an attacker with code execution on the host cannot edit.

Minimize what the crown-jewel system stores, since exfiltration cannot take what was never there. Cl0p's leverage came from the concentration of regulated data in one reachable place. Purge historical records past their retention obligation. Tokenize or vault the highest-sensitivity fields, so an application-level compromise yields references rather than values. Tokenizing replaces a sensitive value with a stand-in that maps back to it only through a separate, protected system. And segment, so that reaching the application does not automatically mean reading everything the database holds.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Which of your ERP systems are reachable from the internet, and why?
check: Can your application servers make arbitrary outbound requests to the internet?
check: Have you timed an emergency patch to your ERP in a drill?
check: Would sustained outbound transfers from your ERP host raise an alert?
check: Would anything flag bulk database reads that no scheduled job explains?
check: Does your ERP still hold records past their retention obligation?
check: Does your incident response plan cover an extortion email that arrives first?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask which ERP systems are internet-facing and why, and whether they sit behind authentication. Ask whether application servers can make arbitrary outbound requests, or only reach an allow-list of destinations.

Ask whether you have measured an emergency ERP patch in a drill: who authorizes it, what the rollback looks like, and how long it takes.

Ask whether sustained outbound transfers from the ERP host, or bulk database reads that no scheduled job explains, would raise an alert, and whether those logs sit where an attacker on the host cannot edit them.

Ask whether the ERP still holds records past their retention obligation, and whether its most sensitive fields are tokenized or vaulted.

Finally, ask whether your incident response plan covers an extortion email that arrives before any alert or CVE.

== An extortion email names your ERP. Who validates it?
layout: question
For many victims, Cl0p's email to executives was the first sign of anything, with no CVE to reference and no indicators published.

Who would validate the claim, and how would you tell what actually left?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page describes a notification chain that ran backwards. For many victims, the extortion email to leadership arrived before Oracle's advisory. The security team was asked to confirm or refute a criminal's claim about a system it had no reason to suspect, with no CVE to reference, no indicators published, and executives already reading a ransom demand.

Ask each person to name who, in their own organization, would take that email first. Then show the answer slide, which gives what the page says to decide in advance.

== What the page says to decide in advance
layout: answer
Write the extortion path into the incident response plan, and rehearse it.
- Who validates the claim, and how you tell what actually left
- When legal and regulatory notification clocks start
- Who communicates, and the position on paying
notes:
The page's answer is to write the extortion path into the incident response plan explicitly.

Assume the first notification arrives as an email to an executive naming a system you have no alert for, with no CVE published. Decide now who validates the claim, how you determine what actually left, when legal and regulatory notification clocks start, and who is authorized to communicate. Also decide the position on paying before a deadline is in front of you.

The page points to the site's incident response page for the general shape, and says this variant needs its own rehearsal. Bring the group's answers back to those four decisions.

== What would you add for stage 01?
layout: question
None of the page's six defenses cites stage 01, the Oracle EBS zero-day advertised at about $70,000 in June 2025.

What would your team do with a listing like that, before any patch existed?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 02 to 07, and none tied to stage 01. In June 2025, dark-web posts advertised an Oracle EBS zero-day at around $70,000, roughly two months before mass exploitation began.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to say what it would have changed while no advisory and no patch existed.

== What the page offers for stage 01
layout: answer
The page's defenses do not cite stage 01. The stage itself calls exploit-market intelligence sometimes the only pre-patch warning available. Any control you propose is your own suggestion; test it against the page's facts.
notes:
Be clear where the page stops. None of its six defenses cites stage 01, and this deck adds none of its own.

What the page does say sits in the stage itself. The public existence of a listing matters to defenders as a timing signal. Threat-intelligence coverage of exploit markets, the page says, is not academic: it is occasionally the only warning that exists during the window when no patch does.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports for stage 01: the June 2025 listing at around $70,000, and exploitation in the wild beginning in August.

